.env
# Private operator/AI notes — threat analysis, exact resource thresholds, etc.
# Never publish: see docs-for-ai/deployment-security-notes.md
# No TRAILING slash, for the reason spelled out on /bills below: a trailing slash matches
# only a real directory, so a SYMLINKED copy of this tree — the way it is shared between
# checkouts — was not ignored and showed up as an ordinary untracked entry. That is the
# worst place in this file for a rule to fail open. Without the slash a symlink and a real
# directory are both ignored. Deliberately left unanchored: matching at any depth is the
# fail-safe direction for a tree that must never be published.
docs-for-ai
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
# No trailing slash for the same reason: a virtualenv is commonly symlinked in.
.venv
# AI assistant state (Claude Code). Ignore the directory's CONTENTS, then re-admit the
# few things a team shares on purpose. This is deny-all-then-re-admit, which `/bills` and
# `tests/data/` above deliberately moved AWAY from -- the polarity is what differs, and it
# decides the answer. There the COMMITTED set is large and grows (every new fixture needed
# a re-admit line, the list reached 80, and a forgotten entry failed silently). Here the
# DISPOSABLE set is what grows: the assistant adds new runtime artifacts over time, while
# the shared set is small and nearly static. Enumerating the disposable half is the side
# that goes stale, and it already did -- the per-machine list this replaces carried eleven
# patterns and still missed `settings.local.json`, which is how #429 was found.
#
# `.claude/*` with the GLOB, never `.claude/` with a trailing slash. Git does not descend
# into an excluded directory, so under the bare-directory form every `!` line below is
# silently dead: the re-admits look right, and `settings.json` stays ignored anyway. The
# glob excludes the entries rather than the directory, which keeps negation working. Same
# class of trap as the trailing-slash note on `/bills` above, in the opposite direction.
#
# `settings.local.json` is the local half of the settings pair: one developer's permission
# allowlist and tool config. It stays ignored by the rule and carries no re-admit -- it
# must not reach a public repo, and committing it would override other contributors'
# settings when they pull. `settings.json` is the shared half and is re-admitted.
#
# Worktrees live here too and must never be committed as gitlinks; the glob covers them.
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.json
!.claude/agents/
!.claude/commands/
!.claude/skills/
# The bill WORKING directory: tools/fetch_bills.py and tools/fetch_bill_text_archives.py download
# into it and it can reach hundreds of MB. Nothing in it is a test input — the curated
# test fixtures live in tests/corpus/ and are tracked normally, so this directory is
# entirely ignored and entirely disposable (#308, ADR 0015). It used to hold both, which
# meant every committed fixture needed a hand-written re-admit line here; that list had
# grown to 80 rules, duplicated tests/corpus_manifest.toml, and failed silently when a
# contributor forgot an entry.
# No TRAILING slash (#319): a trailing slash matches only a real directory, and this is
# commonly SYMLINKED in from another checkout to reuse a downloaded corpus. A symlink is
# not a directory to git, so `bills/` would leave the link untracked and offered for
# commit — recording an absolute path from one developer's machine.
# LEADING slash, though: a pattern with no slash at all is unanchored and matches a path
# component at ANY depth, so a bare `bills` would silently swallow a future `docs/bills/`
# or `webapp/bills/` — reintroducing the silent-`git add` failure #308 exists to remove,
# just somewhere else. `/bills` is anchored to the repo root AND still matches a symlink.
/bills
# govinfo BILLS bulk-text ZIP downloads (tools/fetch_bill_text_archives.py --zip-dir);
# large, re-fetchable. Same anchoring reasoning as above (#319, #308).
/bills_bulk_text
reports/*
!reports/.gitkeep
# LEADING slash (#319): unanchored, this matched a `plans` component at ANY depth, so a
# future tools/plans/ or docs/plans/ would have been ignored with no error.
/plans
# tests/data/ holds committed fixtures (validation JSON, committee-report HTML/PDF, the
# PDF goldens and their source prints), so it is tracked by default and only the
# local-only artifacts are listed here (#308). It used to be deny-all-then-re-admit,
# which put a .gitignore edit in the path of adding a golden.
# Persistent PDF extraction cache (developer test-loop speedup; see tests/pdf_corpus.py)
tests/data/extract_cache/
# The source appropriations spreadsheet is deliberately not vendored; only the JSON
# fixture derived from it is committed.
tests/data/*.xlsx
# Vendored GPO render chain (public domain, ~2.2MB incl. binary JPGs) — local reference only.
# Mined into docs/gpo-render-conventions.md; re-fetch from govinfo bulkdata when needed. See DeltaTrack#40.
# LEADING slash (#319): unanchored, this matched a `reference` component at ANY depth, so a
# future docs/reference/ or src/deltatrack/reference/ would have been ignored with no error.
/reference
# govinfo-derived test corpus for provision-matching research (#170); large, re-derivable.
# No trailing slash: it is commonly symlinked into a worktree root (see #319 above).
bills_corpus

# Generated research render (regenerate via probes/build_artifact.py)
docs/research/provision-matching/paper.html

# Pass 2 (#203) derived mining artifacts — large, re-derivable from bills_corpus/ via the mine_*.py
# probes; they also embed gitignored corpus text. The miners are committed; these regenerate.
docs/research/provision-matching/probes/idf_cache.json
docs/research/provision-matching/probes/candidates_*.json
docs/research/provision-matching/probes/split_assignment.json

# Pass 2 (#203) labeling artifacts — generated per run from the mined candidate pools; they carry
# stratum/split (worklist) or in-progress labels, so they are re-derivable and not committed. The
# self-contained HTML form TEMPLATE is committed; the per-reviewer forms are not.
docs/research/provision-matching/probes/worklist.json
docs/research/provision-matching/probes/worklist_sample.md
docs/research/provision-matching/probes/assignments.json
docs/research/provision-matching/probes/form_*.html
!docs/research/provision-matching/probes/form_template.html
docs/research/provision-matching/probes/labels/
docs/research/provision-matching/probes/merged_labels.json

# Build artifacts. The project became buildable in #398, so `uv build` now produces
# these where it never used to. `build/` matters beyond tidiness: some backends copy
# the package's own .py files into it, and a directory holding Python that is neither
# a discovered product package nor a listed consumer is a deliberate failure in
# tests/test_surface_boundary.py -- so an un-ignored build tree would redden the suite
# for anyone who ran a build.
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/
